==================================================
RECOMMENDED STABLE FRONTDESK FLOW (LAN)
==================================================
Use this if the Sunmi keeps going offline after confirming orders.

Why:
- `npm run usb:recover` only repairs adb/USB temporarily.
- The USB frontdesk flow depends on `adb reverse`.
- If adb goes offline, frontdesk loses Metro/backend access.
- LAN mode removes that dependency for daily use.

One-time frontdesk build for LAN:
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\frontdesk
  $env:EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL='http://192.168.1.228:8000'
  npx expo run:android --port 8082

Daily run after the LAN build:

1) TERMINAL A - BACKEND
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip
  docker compose up -d postgres backend
  Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:8000/health

2) TERMINAL B - FRONTDESK METRO
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\frontdesk
  npx expo start --dev-client --host lan --port 8082 --clear

3) On the Sunmi
  Open the installed frontdesk app.

Expected:
- Frontdesk backend URL is `http://192.168.1.228:8000`
- Metro is served over Wi-Fi/LAN
- adb going offline does not break normal frontdesk connectivity

Rebuild again only if:
- your PC IP changes
- you switch Wi-Fi networks
- native Android files change

Check the PC IP anytime with:
  ipconfig

==================================================
WIRELESS ADB + WIFI BUILD FLOW
==================================================
Yes, you can build/install without keeping USB connected, but `npx expo run:android`
still needs adb access. Over Wi-Fi, that means wireless adb.

Important:
- First setup usually needs one good USB session.
- After wireless adb is enabled, install/build can work over Wi-Fi.
- PC and Sunmi must be on the same Wi-Fi.

Step 1 - Get one clean USB adb connection first
  $ADB='C:\Users\user\AppData\Local\Android\Sdk\platform-tools\adb.exe'
  & $ADB devices -l

If the device is offline:
- unlock the Sunmi
- set USB mode to File Transfer
- accept RSA prompt
- if needed, revoke USB debugging authorizations and re-enable USB debugging

Step 2 - Find the Sunmi Wi-Fi IP
While USB adb is working:
  & $ADB -s V2A4213923214 shell ip addr show wlan0

Look for a line containing `inet`, for example:
  inet 192.168.1.150/24

In that example, the Sunmi IP is:
   192.168.1.212/24

Alternative:
- You can also check the Wi-Fi IP from the Sunmi Wi-Fi settings screen.

Step 3 - Enable wireless adb
  & $ADB -s V2A4213923214 tcpip 5555
  & $ADB connect  192.168.1.212/24:5555
  & $ADB devices -l

Expected:
- one USB device entry may remain
- one Wi-Fi device entry like `<SUNMI_WIFI_IP>:5555` should appear as `device`

Step 4 - Build/install frontdesk over Wi-Fi
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\frontdesk
  $env:EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL='http://192.168.1.228:8000'
  $env:ADB_SERIAL=' 192.168.1.212/24:5555'
  npx expo run:android --port 8082

This uses:
- backend on `192.168.1.228:8000`
- frontdesk Metro/dev port `8082`
- wireless adb for install/build

Step 5 - Daily run after wireless install
Backend:
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip
  docker compose up -d postgres backend

Frontdesk Metro:
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\frontdesk
  $env:ADB_SERIAL=' 192.168.1.212/24:5555'
  npx expo start --dev-client --host lan --port 8082 --clear

Then open the frontdesk app on the Sunmi.

Useful wireless adb commands
Reconnect:
  & $ADB connect  192.168.1.212/24:5555

List devices:
  & $ADB devices -l

Force wireless target:
  $env:ADB_SERIAL=' 192.168.1.212/24:5555'

Disconnect wireless adb:
  & $ADB disconnect  192.168.1.212/24:5555

Notes:
- If the Sunmi changes Wi-Fi network, its IP may change.
- If the IP changes, reconnect using the new IP.
- Wireless adb is for install/debug transport. Your frontdesk app should still use the LAN backend URL:
    http://192.168.1.228:8000

==================================================
A) FULL START (FIRST RUN / AFTER RESTART / AFTER OFFLINE)
==================================================
Open 3 terminals.

1) TERMINAL A - BACKEND
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip
  docker compose up -d postgres backend
  Invoke-RestMethod http://localhost:8000/health

Expected:
  {"status":"ok"}


2) TERMINAL B - MOBILE (8081)
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\mobile
  npx expo start --host lan --port 8081 --clear


3) TERMINAL C - FRONTDESK + USB (8082)
Use this only if you explicitly want the USB reverse workflow.

Set adb vars and recover device:
  $env:ADB_MDNS_OPENSCREEN='0'
  $env:ADB_LIBUSB='0'
  $ADB='C:\Users\user\AppData\Local\Android\Sdk\platform-tools\adb.exe'

  taskkill /F /IM adb.exe 2>$null | Out-Null
  Start-Sleep -Milliseconds 700
  & $ADB start-server
  & $ADB reconnect offline
  & $ADB devices -l

Pick ONLINE device serial:
  $SERIAL = (& $ADB devices | Select-String "device$" | ForEach-Object { ($_ -split "`t")[0] } | Select-Object -First 1)
  $SERIAL
  if ([string]::IsNullOrWhiteSpace($SERIAL)) { throw "No ONLINE adb device found. Fix USB/debugging first." }

Apply reverse map:
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL reverse --remove-all
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL reverse tcp:8081 tcp:8082
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL reverse tcp:8082 tcp:8082
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL reverse tcp:8000 tcp:8000
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL reverse --list

Start Frontdesk Metro:
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\frontdesk
  $env:EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL='http://127.0.0.1:8000'
  npx expo start --dev-client --host lan --port 8082 --clear

Relaunch app on Sunmi:
& "C:\Users\user\AppData\Local\Android\Sdk\platform-tools\adb.exe" -s "V2A4213923214" shell am force-stop com.takeasip.frontdesk
& "C:\Users\user\AppData\Local\Android\Sdk\platform-tools\adb.exe" -s "V2A4213923214" shell monkey -p com.takeasip.frontdesk -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER 1


If screen is still wrong once:
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL shell pm clear com.takeasip.frontdesk
  & $ADB -s $SERIAL shell monkey -p com.takeasip.frontdesk -c android.intent.category.LAUNCHER 1

One-command alternative (auto recover + relaunch):
  cd C:\Users\user\Take-A-Sip\Take-A-Sip\frontdesk
  npm run usb:recover

